Identify the primary uses and historical roles of the Chien Français Blanc et Orange and Cretan Hound in relation to hunting practices
The Chien Français Blanc et Orange is a large French hunting pack hound, primarily used for hunting in packs. It descends from the old Hound of Saintonge type of large hunting dog and is known for its lean and muscular body, long legs, slightly domed head, long drop ears, and slightly square flews. The breed is used for scenting and tracking game, typically working in groups under the direction of a human.

The Cretan Hound is a tracking and coursing hound that was extensively used by the Minoan civilization for chasing and catching hare and wild rabbits on the harsh terrains of Crete. It was praised by classic authors as the best hare-hunters known to man and was exported to Greek colonies and other parts of Europe in ancient times.
