The outcome of the battle was a resounding defeat for Napoleon. His last reserves, the French Imperial Guard, were engaged in a desperate final attack, which was narrowly repelled. Meanwhile, the Prussians broke through on the French right flank, and Wellington's Anglo-allied army launched a counter-attack in the center. This combined assault led to the complete rout of the French army. 

Napoleon abdicated four days later, and on July 7, coalition forces entered Paris, marking the end of his rule as Emperor of the French and the conclusion of his Hundred Days. The Battle of Waterloo effectively ended the Napoleonic Wars and solidified the balance of power in Europe.
