Identify the historical context of the Second Red Scare and determine which legislative acts were enacted during this period to address concerns related to communism and subversion.
The Second Red Scare, occurring after World War II (1939–45), was popularly known as McCarthyism after Senator Joseph McCarthy. This period was marked by widespread fear of domestic subversion and the spread of communism, particularly in the context of the Cold War. Concerns about communist influence led to legislative actions aimed at addressing these fears.

One significant legislative act enacted during this period was the Communist Control Act of 1954. This U.S. federal legislation, signed into law by President Dwight Eisenhower on 24 August 1954, outlawed the Communist Party of the United States and criminalized membership in, or support for, the Party or "Communist-action" organizations. The Act also defined evidence to be considered by a jury in determining participation in the activities, planning, actions, objectives, or purposes of such organizations.

These legislative measures were part of a broader effort to combat perceived threats from communism and subversion during the Second Red Scare.
