Identify the perpetrator of the 2009 Fort Hood mass shooting and provide a detailed account of their background, motivations, and actions leading up to the incident.
The perpetrator of the 2009 Fort Hood mass shooting was Nidal Hasan, a U.S. Army major and psychiatrist. Hasan fatally shot 13 people and injured more than 30 others on November 5, 2009, at Fort Hood, near Killeen, Texas. This incident was the deadliest mass shooting on an American military base.

Hasan's actions were motivated by his extremist views, which included a belief in jihad and a desire to carry out attacks against the United States government. He had previously expressed radical ideas, including the idea of becoming a martyr and targeting American soldiers. His motivations were rooted in his extremist ideology, which he developed over time.

Prior to the shooting, Hasan had been under scrutiny for his behavior and beliefs. He had made comments to coworkers about being a member of Hezbollah and having family connections in al-Qaeda. He also reportedly expressed admiration for the 2009 Fort Hood shooting and its perpetrator, Nidal Hasan (himself). These statements led to increased concern among his colleagues and superiors, who noted his erratic behavior and potential threat to others.

Following the shooting, Hasan was arrested and arraigned by a military court on July 20, 2011, and was charged with 13 counts of premeditated murder and 32 counts of attempted murder under the Uniform Code of Military Justice. His court-martial began on August 7, 2013. During the trial, Hasan was found guilty of all charges, and he was sentenced to life imprisonment without the possibility of parole.
