Analyze the historical context and key events surrounding the establishment, duration, and surrender of this short-lived kingdom.
The short-lived Kingdom of Syria was established in 1920 following the defeat of Ottoman forces in World War I. It was led by Faisal bin Hussein, the son of Sharif Hussein bin Ali. The kingdom claimed territory of a Greater Syria but controlled only a limited area and was dependent on British support. However, Britain and France generally opposed the idea of a Greater Syria and refused to recognize Faisal as king.

The kingdom lasted for about four months, from 8 March to 24 July 1920. Its rule ended after the Battle of Maysalun, where French forces defeated the Arab army under Faisal. As a result, the kingdom surrendered to French forces on 24 July 1920. Following this defeat, Syria became a French Mandate, and Fawzi al-Qawuqji, who had fought in the Battle of Maysalun, later joined the French-Syrian Army. The border between Syria and Iraq, influenced by oil discoveries, was later recognized internationally when Syria became a League of Nations mandate in 1920.
