The historical context of the royal anthem change to "Long Live Our Noble Duke" is rooted in the tradition of Lancashire, where the anthem "God Save the Queen" is modified to show respect for the reigning monarch's title as the Duke of Lancaster. This alteration occurs within the historic county boundaries of Lancashire and involves changing the second line from "Long live our noble Queen" to "Long live our noble Duke." The title "Duke of Lancaster" is always held by the monarch, regardless of gender, and this tradition reflects the historical significance of Lancaster as the county town of Lancashire.

This shift is associated with the era when King George VI was the monarch, specifically in 1951. The change acknowledges the monarch's dual role as both the sovereign and the Duke of Lancaster, highlighting the unique connection between the monarchy and the Duchy of Lancaster. The tradition continues irrespective of the reigning monarch's gender, as seen in the case of Queen Elizabeth II, who was traditionally referred to as the Duke of Lancaster.
