Determine the historical and geographical context of the identified canal to confirm its relevance to the Trust's mission
The historical and geographical context of the Montgomery Canal is significant to the Chester Canal Heritage Trust's mission. Originally constructed in the early 19th century, the Montgomery Canal became part of the Shropshire Union Canal system. It was initially used for transporting goods but fell into disuse following a breach in 1936 and was officially abandoned in 1944. With the revival of canal use in the late 20th century, the canal has been reconnected to the national Canal & River Trust network, though only a section from Frankton Junction to Gronwyn Wharf is currently navigable. The Chester Canal Heritage Trust, established in 1997, promotes the Chester Canal and its heritage, which is historically linked to the broader Shropshire Union Canal system, including the Montgomery Canal. This connection supports the Trust's mission to educate and foster awareness of these waterways and their cultural significance.
