Research the design and engineering influences of the Bajaj Priya to confirm its developmental lineage and source of inspiration.
The Bajaj Priya was a three-speed, 150cc scooter manufactured in Pune, India for Maharashtra Scooters by Bajaj Auto Limited from 1975 until April 1992 under a license agreement with Bajaj Auto Limited. Its design was very similar to that of the earlier "Bajaj 150" model, which itself was based upon the Vespa 150 (VBA type) with a slightly modified body. The Priya shared a headset with the more common Vespa Super, differing from the earlier Vespa and Bajaj 150 models that used fully enclosed headsets. Additionally, the Priya featured a round "volcano" style speedometer, a chromed-plastic Vespa Sprint type taillight, and an angular horncast with a hexagonal Bajaj monogram, distinguishing it from its predecessors. These elements reflect both its lineage from the Vespa 150 and its adaptation to local manufacturing practices and design preferences.
